3. CPU Architecture Requirements
3.1 Registers

The CPU class must include:

8-bit registers: A, F, B, C, D, E, H, L

Alternate set: A’, F’, B’, C’, D’, E’, H’, L’

16-bit registers (derived): AF, BC, DE, HL via getters/setters

Index registers: IX, IY

Special registers: PC, SP, I, R

Internal: halted, interruptsEnabled, tStates

Bus monitor values: currentAddressBus, currentDataBus

Register pairs must be implemented as getters and setters. Example:
get bc() { return (this.b << 8) | this.c; }
set bc(v) { this.b = (v >> 8) & 0xFF; this.c = v & 0xFF; }


3.2 Flags

Flag bits in F register:

Flag	Bit	Meaning
S	7	Sign
Z	6	Zero
H	4	Half-Carry
P/V	2	Parity / Overflow
N	1	Subtract
C	0	Carry

getFlag(mask) { … }
setFlag(mask, value) { … }

Constants:
FLAG_S = 0x80
FLAG_Z = 0x40
FLAG_H = 0x10
FLAG_PV = 0x04
FLAG_N = 0x02
FLAG_C = 0x01

3.3 Memory Model

Memory is a simple flat 64K space:
3.3 Memory Model
new Uint8Array(65536)


Memory Helper Functions
readByte(memory, address)
writeByte(memory, address, value)

3.4 CPU Execution

The CPU operates on instruction boundaries, not clock cycles.

Workflow:

Fetch opcode at PC

Decode opcode via unprefixed table

If prefix (0xCB, 0xED, 0xDD, 0xFD), dispatch to secondary table

Execute handler

Update registers and flags

Update bus values

Example opcode fetch:
const addr = cpu.pc & 0xFFFF;
const opcode = readByte(cpu.memory, addr);
cpu.currentAddressBus = addr;
cpu.currentDataBus = opcode;
cpu.pc = (cpu.pc + 1) & 0xFFFF;
return opcode;

4. Opcode Table System

Each opcode table is an array of 256 entries.

Entry Format

{
  mnemonic: "LD A,n",
  bytes: 2,
  cycles: 7,
  execute(cpu) {
    // perform the instruction
  }
}

Prefix Handling

Unprefixed table’s handler for prefix opcodes must:

Read the next byte

Dispatch to e.g. CB_TABLE[nextByte]

5. UI Requirements

The UI must be simple, legible, and include:

5.1 Register Panel

Show A, F, B, C, D, E, H, L, AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, SP, PC in both hex and binary.

5.2 Flags Panel

Display each flag individually with an indicator showing 0/1.

5.3 Bus Panel

Address bus: show 16-bit binary + hex

Data bus: show 8-bit binary + hex

Values taken from cpu.currentAddressBus and cpu.currentDataBus

5.4 Memory Viewer

Read-only hex viewer

Scrollable
Active address highlight optional

Memory must freeze when simulation is paused

5.5 Controls

Keyboard controls:

Key	Action
Space	Run/pause
S	Single-step
+	Increase CPU speed
-	Decrease CPU speed
File input	Load memory map text file

6. Simulation Loop

Implemented in main.js.

Two options:

Option A — setInterval

Run at adjustable ms interval, perform N CPU steps per tick.

Option B — requestAnimationFrame

Runs every frame, CPU speed determined by number of steps per frame.

In either case:
if running:
    for i in range(stepsPerFrame):
        cpu.step()
ui.render(cpu, memory)


7. Memory Map Loader

Implement in memory.js.

File Format

Each line:
<decimal_address> <hex_byte>

0 3E
1 00
2 3C
3 18
4 FE
5 76

Loader Logic

Skip blank lines.

Skip malformed lines.

Address = decimal 0–65535.

Byte = 2-digit hex.

Overwrite memory[address] = byte.

Hook in HTML

index.html must contain:

<input type="file" id="memoryFileInput">

main.js must handle:
reader.onload = () => {
  loadMemoryMapFromText(memory, reader.result);
  cpu.reset();
  cpu.pc = 0;
};

